    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Silver, mercury and tezontle quarries",
    text: "Silver made New Spain. At Zacatecas, Guanajuato, Taxco and Pachuca, miners (barreteros) broke ore with iron bars and blasting powder, and carriers (tenateros) hauled it up notched poles in leather bags, while refining in the patio process mixed crushed ore with mercury, salt and water, trodden by mules for weeks. The work killed many through collapse, flooding and mercury poisoning. Mine labour was mostly paid wage work with a share of ore (partido). Near the capital, quarrymen cut the red volcanic stone tezontle and grey chiluca that face its churches and palaces, and lime burners supplied mortar. Salt pans and saltpetre works supplied colonial industry.",
    wiki: ["Patio process", "Mining in Mexico", "Tezontle"],
