    text: "Urban crafts in New Spain were organised into guilds (gremios) on the Spanish model, each with ordinances approved by the city council. An apprentice was bound by contract for several years, became a journeyman (oficial) and could open his own shop only after passing the examen de maestro before guild inspectors, though rules barring Indigenous people and castas were often ignored in practice. Each guild had a cofradía with a patron saint, such as Saint Eligius for silversmiths, which marched in the Corpus Christi procession and helped members' widows. Alongside the guild shops stood obrajes, textile workshops where debtors and convicts were locked in to weave wool.",
