    text: "Mexico City's Plaza Mayor held two worlds of trade: the Parián, a walled market of shops selling Asian silks and porcelain brought by the Manila galleon to Acapulco, and around it an open tianguis where Indigenous women sold produce, flowers and cooked food as their ancestors had. Cacao beans still served as small change well into the colonial period, since coins were scarce, and shopkeepers issued their own tokens (tlacos). Goods moved inland by arrieros, mule drivers leading trains of pack mules along the royal roads (caminos reales) to the mines and ports, and by Indigenous cargadores who carried loads on their backs in the city.",
