    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Cabildos, repúblicas de indios and the Gazeta",
    text: "New Spain was governed through councils. Spanish towns had a cabildo of regidores and alcaldes, while Indigenous towns were organised as repúblicas de indios with their own elected governor, alcaldes and scribes who kept records in Nahuatl, Mixtec or Maya and collected tribute for the crown. The viceroy and the Audiencia sat in the capital. News spread by town criers (pregoneros) reading edicts in the plaza, by sermons, by letters carried on the mule roads, and, from 1722, by the Gazeta de México, the first newspaper of the Americas. Rumour moved fastest: the arrival of the fleet at Veracruz or news of a war with England reached the city in days.",
