    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Vecindades, adobe and the congregaciones",
    text: "In Mexico City most people rented. Poor families of every casta lived in vecindades, courtyards lined with one-room dwellings sharing a well or fountain, owned by convents or wealthy landlords; a household might be a widow, her children and a lodger. Houses of adobe on stone footings needed replastering after every rainy season, and the city's soft lakebed made walls lean and crack. In the countryside, Spanish policy moved scattered Indigenous hamlets into planned grid towns around a church and plaza, the congregaciones of the 1550s to 1610s, so that many families had to build new homes where officials put them. Houses and house plots passed by testament, often to daughters as well as sons.",
    wiki: ["Vecindad", "Reductions", "Adobe"],
