    title: "The alhóndiga, the pósito and the maize riot of 1692",
    text: "Colonial towns tried to guarantee bread and maize. Growers had to sell grain through the alhóndiga, a municipal exchange where prices were posted, and the pósito kept a reserve to sell cheaply in dear years. Households bought maize by the almud and stored it in jars and sacks; the city poor lived day to day. When rains failed in 1691 and a blight struck wheat, prices soared, and on 8 June 1692 a crowd angry at hoarding burned the viceroy's palace and the market stalls. A family that fell on hard times pawned clothes and tools at the corner shop (pulpería), sought help from its confraternity, or moved in with kin.",
