    text: "Marriage in New Spain was a Catholic sacrament. The couple went before the parish priest, who took sworn statements (diligencias matrimoniales) that they were free to marry, not close kin and consenting, and read the banns on three Sundays. Church law let children marry against their parents' wishes, and many did, until the crown's Royal Pragmatic of 1776, extended to the Americas in 1778, let parents block unequal matches. Elite families gave daughters dowries in cash, land and goods; poorer brides might get one from a confraternity's charity lottery. Marriages between Spaniards, Indigenous people and Africans produced the castas, and many couples simply lived together without a church wedding.",
