    text: "Every child was to learn Christian doctrine. In Indigenous towns, children gathered at the church each morning where a fiscal drilled them in prayers and catechism, often in Nahuatl or another native language. In the cities, small girls attended an amiga, a woman who taught prayers, sewing and sometimes reading in her home, and boys whose parents could afford it went to schools of reading and writing, then to the Jesuit colleges. Most children learned work at home. Parents placed sons of ten to fourteen with a master by notarised contract, promising food, clothes and teaching in return for years of service.",
