    text: "Under Spanish law a widow recovered her dowry and took half the property the couple had acquired during the marriage (gananciales), and she often became guardian of her children and head of the household. Widows ran shops, workshops and haciendas; in Mexico City a large share of households were headed by women. Old parents usually lived with a married child, and a testament made before a notary laid out who would care for them and inherit. Indigenous men over fifty were freed from tribute. The destitute old could turn to hospitals run by religious orders or to their confraternity, which paid for a decent burial.",
