    text: "A toothache in colonial Mexico went to the barber. Barbers were licensed by the Protomedicato, the royal board that examined physicians, surgeons, apothecaries and barbers, and they drew teeth and let blood from shops marked by a basin sign. Most people never saw a licensed practitioner. Indigenous and mestizo curanderos kept the old remedies of the Badianus manuscript: hot chile or tobacco applied to the gum, herbs chewed or packed around the tooth, and smoke. Friars printed herbal remedies for their missions. Rotting teeth were common among the wealthy, who ate the sugar and chocolate that New Spain produced.",
