    text: "Colonial Mexicans went to law constantly. Minor disputes over insults, debts, straying animals or water were heard by the town alcalde, Spanish or Indigenous. From 1592 Indigenous people could bring cases cheaply to the Juzgado General de Indios in Mexico City, the General Indian Court, whose fees were covered by a small tax; villages sued estates and each other over land, and neighbours sued neighbours. The Church courts handled marriage, slander and morals. Parish priests and elders often mediated before anyone filed. Petitions written in Nahuatl by town scribes survive in thousands.",
