    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Footploughs, terraces and desert canals",
    text: "Andean fields were worked with the chakitaqlla, a footplough of wood with a foot rest and later a bronze tip, used by a line of men turning sod while women followed breaking clods and placing seed. In the highlands, potatoes, oca, quinoa and maize were grown on stepped terraces (andenes) and ridged fields, and the land was rotated with long fallows. On the desert coast nothing grew without canals: Moche and later Chimú farmers took water from rivers through channels tens of kilometres long to maize, beans, cotton and squash, fertilised with fish and seabird guano. Fields belonged to the ayllu, and families received plots by need.",
