    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Llamas and alpacas on the puna and the coast",
    text: "The Andes were the only part of the Americas with herds. Llamas carried loads of 25 to 30 kilograms and gave meat, dung for fuel and coarse wool; alpacas gave fine wool. Herding was done mostly by girls, boys and young women on the high grasslands of the puna, where crops would not grow, and herders moved animals between wet-season and dry-season pastures. Herds were marked with coloured tassels at a ceremony at the height of the rains. The Moche kept llamas on the coast too, and llama bones and dung are common at their sites. Under the Inca, herds belonged to the state, the gods and the ayllus, and were counted on the khipu.",
