    text: "Wild resources mattered at every altitude. On the puna, hundreds of people joined in the chaku, a great drive that herded wild vicuña and guanaco into a ring of people or corral to be shorn and released, with some killed; under the Inca only the state could order one. Deer, viscacha and birds were hunted with slings and bolas. Along the Pacific, the cold Humboldt Current made one of the richest fisheries on earth. Fishermen went out astride caballitos de totora, small reed boats, to net anchovy and sardine and gathered shellfish and seaweed; fishing villages traded dried fish inland for farm produce. On Lake Titicaca, people fished and hunted waterfowl from larger reed boats.",
