    text: "Andean people mined and gathered what the land held. At Maras, near Cusco, a salty spring was led into hundreds of small evaporation pans, which families still work. Gold was washed from river gravels, and silver and copper were mined in shallow pits and smelted in wind-blown clay furnaces (huayra) on windy hillsides. On the islands off the coast, workers dug the thick deposits of seabird guano to fertilise fields; the Inca assigned islands to particular valleys and, by later report, punished anyone who disturbed the birds in the breeding season. Stone for fine walls was split from quarries such as Kachiqhata above Ollantaytambo and dragged down ramps.",
