    text: "Most Andean craft was done at home, especially spinning and weaving, which every woman did. But the finest work came from specialists. At Cerro Mayal near the Moche capital, potters used moulds to turn out figurines and stirrup-spout vessels in quantity. Metalworkers at the Moche lords' courts gilded copper by electrochemical replacement. Under the Inca, the finest cloth, cumbi, was woven by male specialists (cumbicamayoq) and by the aqllakuna, chosen women cloistered in houses in provincial centres; the Inca gave it as the highest gift. Craft skills passed within families and ayllus, and before a firing or a weaving, craftspeople made offerings of chicha and coca to the powers of the clay and the loom.",
