    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Exchange without markets: the vertical archipelago",
    text: "The Andes had no money and, except in a few places, no marketplaces. Instead families and ayllus spread out to reach different altitudes: a highland community sent its own people to farm maize in warm valleys, herd on the puna and gather on the coast, an arrangement the anthropologist John Murra called the vertical archipelago. What could not be grown was obtained by barter (trueque) between kin and partners, measure for measure, such as dried potatoes for maize or wool for chile. Goods moved by llama caravans and on human backs, men with loads tied in a carrying cloth. On the Chincha coast specialist traders exchanged spondylus shell (mullu), prized for offerings, from Ecuador.",
    wiki: ["Vertical archipelago", "John Victor Murra", "Spondylus"],
