    evidence: "inferred",
    title: "Makers' marks on the Huaca del Sol",
    text: "Public work in the Andes was a labour tax, not wages. At the Huaca del Sol, the largest adobe mound in South America, the millions of bricks carry makers' marks, dozens of distinct signs that archaeologists read as the tallies of work gangs from different communities, each owing its share of bricks. Later the Inca made this system universal as mit'a, rotating turns of service in fields, roads and armies, supervised by kurakas and recorded on knotted cords. News travelled by runners. Moche pottery shows runners carrying small bags, which some have argued held lima beans marked with signs, a kind of message, though this remains unproven.",
