    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Llama dung, ichu grass and stone channels",
    text: "Above the tree line, where many Andean people lived, there was little wood. Families burned taquia, dried llama and alpaca dung, gathered in baskets from corrals and pastures, along with bundles of ichu grass and the resinous cushion plant yareta, which grows a centimetre a year. Wood from queñua and valley trees was precious and went to building. On the coast, algarrobo trees fed the fires. Water came from springs, lakes and snowmelt, and the Inca were masters of channelling it: at Cusco, Tipón and Machu Picchu stone-lined channels led water to a series of fountains where people filled jars. Springs themselves were huacas, sacred places given offerings before cleaning the canals each year.",
