    text: "Andean households got by through exchanges of labour. Ayni was help returned in kind: you worked on my field for a day and I owed you a day on yours, remembered exactly. Minka was a work party called by a household or the ayllu, with the host feeding workers and serving chicha. Debts were counted in days of work rather than money, and a family that could not return help lost standing. After a frost or a poor harvest, kin and the ayllu shared seed and food. Under the Inca, the state's storehouses (qollqa), lined up on hillsides by the thousand, held freeze-dried potato (chuño), maize and cloth to feed armies and relieve famine, but households were meant to recover through their ayllu first.",
