    evidence: "inferred",
    title: "The first haircut and children at work in the Andes",
    text: "Andean children were not fully named until the rutuchikuy, the first haircut, held at about two years old, when relatives each cut a lock of hair and gave the child a gift of a llama, cloth or a field, beginning its own property. The ceremony is known from Inca-period accounts and is still practised, and something like it may be older. Children learned by working from about five: scaring birds from the fields, fetching water and fuel, and herding, which was children's work above all. Girls learned to spin with a drop spindle while walking, and weaving at eight or nine. Guaman Poma drew Inca age grades from infant to old age, each with its duties.",
