    evidence: "inferred",
    title: "Trial marriage and the couple as a working pair",
    text: "In Andean belief, a person became a full adult only when married, since farming, herding and weaving took a man and a woman together (qhari-warmi, 'man-woman'). A couple often lived together first in a trial marriage, known in later Quechua as sirvinakuy, 'serving each other', during which families watched whether the two could work well together; separation was possible before children came. Marriage was usually within the ayllu. Under the Inca, officials were said to pair off young people of an age in each district. On the coast, among the Moche, burials of men and women together show couples buried side by side, but their customs are known only from graves and pottery.",
