    text: "In the Andes the old kept working as long as they could: Guaman Poma's drawings of Inca age grades show old men twisting rope and minding houses and old women spinning, weaving and caring for children, with those too weak to work fed from the community's fields. Old people's knowledge of ritual, weather and land rights made them respected. A widow or widower kept access to ayllu land, and neighbours and kin were supposed to plant and harvest it for them under the rule of mutual help; the Inca assigned labour to widows' fields before the ruler's own, according to Garcilaso de la Vega. Remarriage was common, and the dead remained present as mallquis consulted by the family.",
