    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Guild workshops and Indigenous masters in Lima and Cusco",
    text: "Spanish crafts reached the Andes with Spanish guild rules: apprenticeship by contract, the grade of journeyman and the examination for master. In practice Indigenous and mestizo artisans dominated many trades. In Cusco, native painters broke from the Spanish painters' guild in 1688 and developed the Cusco School of religious painting, while Indigenous silversmiths, sculptors and carpenters filled churches with work. Potosí had hundreds of silversmiths and blacksmiths serving the mines. Each craft kept a confraternity with a patron saint whose feast it celebrated with a mass and procession; in Andean towns the offering to the saint often sat beside a quiet one to Pachamama.",
