    scope: COL_ANDES,
    topics: [{ workplace: "market" }, { workplace: "carrying" }, { goal: "barter" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Gateras in the qhatu and the llama trains of Potosí",
    text: "Potosí, with perhaps 150,000 people by 1610, created a vast market economy in the Andes. In its Indigenous market, the qhatu, women called gateras sold coca, chicha, maize, potatoes, cloth and fuel, and some grew rich; coca, grown in the eastern valleys, was the great commodity, bought by miners by the basket. Much small exchange was still barter, and silver ore itself served as money. Goods arrived by llama caravans driven by Indigenous herders from as far as the coast, and by Spanish mule trains from Argentina and Lima; silver bars left for Arica and the Pacific fleet. Carrying was also women's work, with loads bundled in woven cloths (llicllas) on the back.",
