    topics: [{ workplace: "civic" }, { goal: "news" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Corregidores, caciques and town criers",
    text: "Colonial Andean government worked through two sets of officers. The Spanish corregidor ruled a province, collected tribute and, notoriously, forced goods on Indigenous people at high prices under the reparto de mercancías. Beneath him, hereditary caciques or kurakas and elected Indigenous alcaldes ran the reducciones, organised the mita contingents and settled local disputes. Protectors of natives represented Indigenous communities in the courts. News came from pulpits and town criers, from mule drivers, and from letters; rumour could be powerful, as in the spread of the Taki Unquy movement in the 1560s and of Túpac Amaru II's rebellion in 1780–81.",
