    text: "Catholicism took root in the Andes through saints and confraternities. Every town had a patron saint whose fiesta was sponsored by a cargo-holder (alférez or mayordomo), who spent heavily on masses, fireworks, food and chicha and gained prestige. The Virgin of Copacabana, carved by the Indigenous sculptor Francisco Tito Yupanqui in 1583, drew pilgrims from across the highlands. Priests tried to root out the old huacas; campaigns of the 'extirpation of idolatry' in the seventeenth century burned mallquis and punished ritual specialists. Many people continued to honour mountains and Pachamama alongside the saints.",
