    evidence: "documented",
    title: "From doctrina child to reservado: the tribute ages",
    text: "A colonial Andean life was measured by tribute. Children attended the doctrina, where an Indigenous fiscal gathered them daily for catechism in Quechua or Aymara, and learned farming, herding and weaving from parents. Sons of caciques might go to Jesuit colleges for native nobles in Lima and Cusco. At eighteen a man became a tributary, owing tribute and his turns of mita. At fifty he became a reservado, freed from both, and old parents lived with a married child, usually the youngest son, who inherited the house. The old kept authority as ritual experts and memory keepers of land boundaries, and their testimony settled land lawsuits.",
