    text: "A mitayo usually went to Potosí with his wife and children, and the family depended on both: the mine wage was too low to live on, so wives brewed chicha, sold in the qhatu and wove cloth. Debts piled up at the shops and pawnbrokers where tools, clothing and silver ornaments were pledged. Communities that could not send their quota paid silver instead to hire replacements. Many households escaped the mita by leaving their ayllu altogether and becoming forasteros, outsiders in another community without land rights but exempt from the draft; by 1690 forasteros were about half the population in many provinces. Recovery came from kin networks and the ayllu's lands when a family could return.",
