    text: "In Lima and Potosí, licensed barbers and surgeons pulled teeth, under the supervision of the Protomedicato, the royal medical board. In the highlands people relied on Indigenous healers and on coca, which Spanish writers noted was used to relieve toothache and strengthen the gums, and on the herbal knowledge of the Kallawaya. Sugar, newly available through colonial trade, and chicha wore on teeth. The Jesuits' pharmacies sold remedies, and friars recorded native plants such as quinine bark for fever. For most people a bad tooth meant chewing coca until it was pulled.",
