    text: "Andean people used Spanish courts vigorously. Quarrels between neighbours were heard by Indigenous alcaldes or the cacique, and serious cases went to the corregidor. Communities hired Spanish lawyers and sent delegations to the Audiencias of Lima and Charcas to sue over land, tribute and abuse, often winning. The protector de naturales pleaded for Indigenous clients free of charge. The khipu survived as a legal record: in the early colonial period knot-keepers read their cords in court as evidence of tribute paid or goods taken. Priests and elders also mediated, and a quarrel could be made up publicly by sharing chicha at a fiesta.",
