    title: "Chert hoes and maize fields in the American Bottom",
    text: "Mississippian farming was women's work, done with hoes whose blades were large flakes of Mill Creek chert from quarries in southern Illinois, hafted on wooden handles; the blades wore to a glassy polish and were traded widely. After about 900 CE, maize became the main crop, alongside native seed plants such as goosefoot, maygrass and knotweed, squash, sunflower and later beans. Fields spread across the rich floodplain of the American Bottom around Cahokia and on terraces nearby. There were no herds of any kind; the dog was the only domestic animal, so no one did pasture work. Men cleared land, hunted and built, and the whole town turned out for planting and harvest rites.",
