    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Deer, backwater fish and swans on the Mississippi",
    text: "The Mississippi floodplain was rich in wild food. Men hunted white-tailed deer with bow and arrow, sometimes in communal drives, and supplied venison and hides to towns; elite households at Cahokia received choice cuts, which archaeologists can detect in their refuse. The oxbow lakes, sloughs and backwaters teemed with fish, caught with nets, weirs, hooks and spears: gar, bowfin, catfish and drum. Waterfowl, including swans, were taken on the flyway, and their feathers went into fans and cloaks. Women and children gathered hickory nuts, pecans, persimmons, berries and wild greens. Dugout canoes, hollowed from cypress or cottonwood with fire and stone adze, carried both fishermen and goods.",
