    text: "Mississippian crafts ranged from everyday to sacred. Workers quarried Mill Creek and Dover chert for hoes and knives, dug red flint clay, and potters tempered their clay with crushed mussel shell, which made thin, strong vessels. At Cahokia, specialists made thousands of beads from marine shell brought from the Gulf Coast; Mound 72 held a cape of some 20,000 of them. Copper from the Great Lakes was hammered and embossed into plates showing falcon-dancers and long-nosed god earpieces, work probably done in special buildings under ritual control. Such skills seem to have been taught within lineages, and objects made for the gods were thought to hold power of their own.",
