    title: "Canoe routes, chunkey and feasts at Cahokia",
    text: "Cahokia sat where the Missouri, Illinois and Mississippi rivers meet, and goods moved by canoe and on foot along them: shell from the Gulf, copper from Lake Superior, mica from the Appalachians, salt from southern Illinois. There were no markets or money; exchange ran through gifts between chiefs, feasts and kin ties. Great feasts drew thousands, and deposits of butchered deer, broken pots and tobacco seeds in the Grand Plaza show them. News and neighbourly life circulated at such gatherings and at chunkey games, where players rolled a stone disc and threw poles after it while crowds bet on the outcome; the game spread from Cahokia across the Southeast and was still played in the 1700s.",
