    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Hominy, storage pits and the price of maize",
    text: "A Mississippian household cooked over an indoor or yard hearth, burning wood cut from the bluffs and floodplain forests, which Cahokia's growth thinned noticeably. Women pounded maize in wooden mortars and cooked it as hominy and mush with beans, squash, nuts and venison, and water was carried from springs, creeks and the river in shell-tempered jars. Surplus maize was kept in raised granaries and in pits dug inside or beside houses, sealed against damp and vermin. The new diet had a cost that skeletons record clearly: sticky, starchy maize caused tooth decay, abscesses and tooth loss far more often than among earlier foragers, and a toothache was a common misery.",
