    text: "Around 1050 CE Cahokia grew suddenly into a town of perhaps 10,000 to 15,000 people, and strontium isotopes in teeth show that a third or more were immigrants from elsewhere in the Midwest and South. Newcomers built the new style of house: poles set in a dug trench rather than individual postholes, bent or tied to a roof frame, walled with cane matting and daub and thatched with grass. Houses were rebuilt every decade or so as posts rotted, often on the same spot, and whole neighbourhoods were razed and replanned when the town's leaders reorganised space. A house was a family's place, but land and position depended on belonging to a kin group.",
