    evidence: "hypothesis",
    title: "Matrilineal clans and a Mississippian childhood",
    text: "No Mississippian people left written records of family life, but their descendants in the Southeast, such as the Creek, Cherokee, Choctaw and Natchez, were organised into matrilineal clans, and most scholars think Mississippian societies were too. A child belonged to its mother's clan, marriage within the clan was forbidden, and a husband often moved to his wife's family's house. Her brothers, not her husband, disciplined and taught her sons. Girls learned farming, pottery and cooking from mothers and grandmothers; boys learned to hunt, make tools and play chunkey. The Natchez in the 1700s had noble and commoner ranks whose children had to marry across them, a possible echo of older rules.",
