    text: "Mississippian peoples often laid out their dead in charnel houses on mound tops, cleaned the bones after the flesh decayed and later buried them together in bundles, so mourning was long and staged. At Cahokia's Mound 72, around 1050 CE, a man was laid on a bird-shaped platform of shell beads, surrounded by retainers and by pits of young women who had been killed, showing the terrible power of elite funerals. Ordinary people were buried in cemeteries near their homes. Among later Southeastern peoples, widows mourned for months with unbound hair, and elders, especially old women of the clan, held authority in family and council.",
