    title: "The falcon dancer, Woodhenge and the busk",
    text: "Mississippian religion is known from art, sites and later Southeastern tradition. Artists carved and embossed a falcon-costumed figure, the Birdman, often identified with the hero Morning Star, and a cosmos of Upper, Middle and Lower Worlds. At Cahokia, rings of tall cedar posts, dubbed Woodhenge, marked the solstices and equinoxes from which the ritual year was set. Temples on mound tops held sacred fires. Later Creek and Cherokee peoples held the busk, or Green Corn Ceremony, when the new maize ripened, putting out all fires and relighting them from a new sacred flame, fasting, cleansing and forgiving wrongs. Something like this probably marked the Mississippian year too.",
