    text: "In a Mississippian town, a family that lost its harvest to flood or drought turned to its clan, whose members were bound to feed and house each other. Chiefs gathered tribute in maize and hides and gave it back at feasts, which made them both redistributors and creditors of loyalty. There was no money, so every debt was a social one. Among later Southeastern peoples, killings were settled by the principle of clan retaliation: the victim's clan was owed a life or compensation, and the Green Corn Ceremony was the time when lesser wrongs could be forgiven. Such customs are recorded from the 1700s and are used by archaeologists to imagine earlier practice.",
