    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Clam gardens, root beds and Haida tobacco",
    text: "The peoples of the Northwest Coast kept no herds and had no maize, but they cultivated more than was once thought. Families owned estuarine gardens of springbank clover and Pacific silverweed roots, weeded and dug with yew sticks, and built clam gardens: rock walls at the low-tide line that trapped sediment and raised clam yields. The Haida and Tlingit grew a native tobacco in plots, mixed with lime from burned shell and chewed. Berry patches were burned and pruned. On land, men hunted deer, black bear and mountain goat with bows, snares and deadfalls, and women gathered berries, camas and cedar bark in family-owned places passed down by lineage.",
