    title: "Halibut hooks, cedar canoes and fresh streams",
    text: "Most of a Northwest Coast year's work was on the water. Men fished halibut from canoes on offshore banks with V-shaped hooks of bent wood and bone, often carved with spirit figures, took salmon at weirs and traps in family-owned streams, raked herring and eulachon with toothed rakes, and hunted seals, sea lions and, among the Nuu-chah-nulth, whales. Haida dugout canoes, carved from single red cedar logs up to 20 metres long, could carry forty people and tonnes of goods across Hecate Strait. Villages stood on beaches near fresh streams, from which women carried drinking water in watertight baskets and wooden boxes; hot stones dropped into the boxes boiled the water for cooking.",
