    text: "Red cedar was the Northwest Coast's great material. Men split planks from standing trees with wedges of antler and yew, felled trees for canoes and totem poles, and shaped them with adzes of stone and later of trade iron; women stripped bark for mats, clothing and rope. Master carvers were ranked specialists, commissioned and paid by chiefs, and a young man learned by working for years under his uncle or another master, first roughing out and then finishing. Before cutting a tree, carvers asked its permission and made offerings. From the 1820s Haida carvers also quarried black argillite at Slatechuck Creek for carvings sold to sailors. Canoes carried timber, goods and people between villages.",
