    title: "Smoke, drying racks and bentwood boxes",
    text: "Inside a Haida plank house, often with a sunken central floor, several related families each kept a hearth, and the work of the household was preserving food for winter. Women split and dried salmon on racks in the smoke under the roof, rendered eulachon into grease, dried halibut, seaweed and berry cakes, and packed them into steam-bent cedar boxes, whose sides were bent from a single kerfed plank and sewn. Firewood was driftwood and split cedar, gathered constantly; the fires burned all day for cooking and smoking. Checking the boxes for mould and measuring what remained against the winter months was the house mistress's responsibility, since a bad spring meant hunger before the fish returned.",
