    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Raven and Eagle: marriage and the uncle's house",
    text: "Every Haida person belonged by birth to their mother's moiety, Raven or Eagle, and to a lineage within it, and had to marry someone of the other moiety. Marriages among the high-ranking were arranged by families to join lineages of equal standing, with gifts from the groom's side and a return of goods from the bride's; commoners had more choice. A child's lineage, crests and inheritance came through the mother, so the man who mattered most to a boy was his mother's brother. At about ten a boy went to live in his uncle's house to learn the lineage's stories, rights and skills, and one day to inherit its name. Girls learned food preservation and weaving from mothers.",
