    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Elders, widows and names that do not die",
    text: "On the Northwest Coast, old age brought authority: elders held the lineage's histories, songs, crests and fishing rights in memory and taught them to heirs, and the house chief was usually a senior man. Old women advised on marriages and supervised food preparation. The old were fed from the house stores and honoured at feasts. When a husband died, his widow belonged to a different lineage and moiety, so his heir, often his sister's son, took his name and property; custom among the Haida and Tlingit expected the heir to marry the widow, so that she was not left without support. Widows cut their hair and fasted in mourning.",
    wiki: ["Tlingit", "Haida people", "Levirate marriage"],
