    scope: PNW,
    topics: [{ goal: "worship" }, { occasion: "day.patron-offering" }, { occasion: "day.toothache" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Salmon people, crests and the sg̱aaga",
    text: "Northwest Coast religion saw animals as people in another form. Salmon were believed to be a people who gave their bodies each year, and the first salmon of the season was welcomed with a ceremony and its bones returned to the water so that they could be reborn. Crests such as Raven, Eagle, Bear or Killer Whale were ancestral beings a lineage had encountered, and honouring them was honouring one's own origins. Illness, including pain in the teeth or jaw, was treated by the shaman (sg̱aaga in Haida), who went into trance to find and remove an intruding object or recover a lost soul, using carved rattles and charms, alongside women's herbal cures such as devil's club.",
    wiki: ["Haida mythology", "Oplopanax horridus", "Shamanism among Alaska Natives"],
