    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Quill societies, the pipestone quarry and the travois",
    text: "Among the Lakota, women's crafts were ranked and sacred. Quillwork, embroidering hides with dyed and flattened porcupine quills, was taught by accomplished women, and quillers formed societies whose members made vows and feasts; the craft was a gift from Double Woman, a powerful spirit, and a woman's quilled robes were counted like a man's war honours. Beadwork with trade beads spread in the 1800s. Red pipestone (catlinite), for sacred pipes, came from a quarry in what is now southwestern Minnesota, where people of many nations dug by hand under a truce. Everything a family owned was carried on horse and dog travois, two poles dragged behind the animal with a platform lashed between them.",
