    title: "Tobacco ties, the pipe and the pejuta wičháša",
    text: "Lakota people addressed the sacred through Wakȟáŋ Tȟáŋka, the great mystery, and the powers of the directions, the earth, the bison and the thunder beings. A person seeking help filled the pipe and offered it to the sky, earth and four winds, or tied pinches of tobacco in small cloth bundles as offerings, hung at a sacred place. A spirit helper met in a vision quest remained a lifelong patron. For sickness, including toothache, people went to a pejuta wičháša, a medicine man skilled in roots and herbs, who might give a plant such as purple coneflower (echinacea), still known as a remedy for toothache and sore throat, or to a holy man for a spiritual cure.",
