    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Marajoara urns, Santarém pottery and stone axes",
    text: "Amazonian peoples made some of the finest pottery in the ancient Americas. On Marajó Island, from about 400 to 1400 CE, potters built elaborate painted and incised burial urns and figures, and at Santarém on the Tapajós they modelled vessels crowded with animal figures. Potters, usually women, dug clay from riverbanks and tempered it with burned tree bark (cariapé) or sponge spicules (cauixi). Stone was scarce on the floodplain, so axe heads and grinding stones were traded from distant outcrops and highly valued. Skills passed from mother to daughter, and many peoples believed the clay had a mother spirit to be asked before digging and before firing, when breaking was feared.",
    wiki: ["Marajoara culture", "Santarém culture", "Pre-Columbian art"],
