    evidence: "documented",
    title: "River chiefdoms, canoe trade and village splits",
    text: "When Orellana's expedition descended the Amazon in 1542, the friar Gaspar de Carvajal described long stretches of riverbank villages under powerful chiefs, such as Machiparo and the Omagua, with roads, storehouses and warriors; epidemics soon destroyed them. Trade ran by canoe along the rivers: pottery, stone, feathers, salt, curare and shell beads passed between peoples, often through ritual trading partners and at intertribal feasts where news was exchanged. Carrying on land used baskets with tumplines. Quarrels between neighbours were handled by the headman's persuasion, public speeches and, among some peoples, ritual duels with clubs or chest-pounding; when disputes could not be settled, a faction left to found a new village.",
    wiki: ["Gaspar de Carvajal", "Omagua people", "Francisco de Orellana"],
