    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Manioc in the ground and farinha in baskets",
    text: "Bitter manioc was the Amazonian food store. Roots could be left in the ground for a year or more and dug when needed, so the garden itself was the granary. Processed manioc, grated, squeezed of its poison in a woven tube press (tipiti) and toasted on a clay griddle, became farinha and beiju cakes that kept for months in leaf-lined baskets hung in the house. Smoked fish and meat were dried on racks. A household with a failed garden or no hunter was fed by its kin, since meat especially had to be shared, and a family that took without giving lost standing. There was no money or interest; exchange created lasting obligations between families and villages.",
    wiki: ["Cassava", "Tapioca", "Farofa"],
