    evidence: "inferred",
    title: "Shamans, burial urns and the spirit masters",
    text: "Amazonian peoples lived among spirits: the masters of game and fish who had to be respected, and the souls of plants and animals. The shaman (pajé in Tupi) travelled to the spirit world with tobacco smoke, snuffs or ayahuasca to bargain for game, find lost souls and cure. Illness, a toothache included, was often blamed on a spirit dart or sorcery, which the shaman sucked out and showed. Offerings of smoke, drink and restraint in hunting kept the masters content. At death, many peoples mourned loudly, cut their hair and later reburied the bones, as in the painted Marajoara urns. A widow often returned to her own kin or married her husband's brother.",
    wiki: ["Pajé", "Ayahuasca", "Marajoara culture"],
