    topics: [{ occasion: "day.patron-offering" }, { occasion: "day.craft-power" }, { occasion: "day.toothache" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "No saints: providence, the calling and Cotton Mather's cures",
    text: "Protestant colonists rejected saints and their shrines. A New England Puritan or a Virginia Anglican asked favour for his work by private prayer to God alone, reading good weather, a safe voyage or a sickness as providence, and kept fast days in trouble and thanksgiving days after deliverance. Work itself was a 'calling' to be pursued diligently. Illness was both a trial from God and something to treat. Cotton Mather's medical book 'The Angel of Bethesda', finished in 1724, mixes spiritual reflection with remedies, including for toothache. Teeth were pulled by barbers, smiths or itinerant tooth-drawers; later, in the 1760s, Paul Revere fitted false teeth carved from ivory in Boston.",
    wiki: ["Cotton Mather", "Providentialism", "Paul Revere"],
