    topics: [{ workplace: "wild" }, { workplace: "water" }, { workplace: "extraction" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Lake fishing, ice harvests and limestone quarries",
    text: "Around the industrial cities of the Great Lakes, older outdoor work continued. Commercial fishermen, many Scandinavian, set gill nets for whitefish, lake trout and chubs on Lake Michigan from tugs, and catches declined as overfishing and, later, sea lamprey took hold. Each winter, crews cut ice from lakes and ponds with horse-drawn ice ploughs and saws and packed it in sawdust in huge icehouses to supply city iceboxes, until mechanical refrigeration ended the trade in the 1920s and 30s. Quarrymen cut limestone at Joliet and Lemont, and coal came from southern Illinois. Hunting was becoming sport rather than livelihood, regulated by state licences and game wardens from the early 1900s.",
    wiki: ["Ice trade", "Lake Michigan", "Joliet, Illinois"],
