    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Maxwell Street, South Water Market and the teamsters",
    text: "In the industrial city, buying and selling ranged from department stores on State Street to street markets. On Maxwell Street, on Chicago's Near West Side, Jewish immigrant peddlers and later Black and Mexican vendors sold clothes, tools and food from pushcarts and stalls every day but the Sabbath, bargaining loudly. Wholesale produce came through the crowded South Water Market. Goods moved by rail into the city's freight yards and were then hauled by teamsters with horse wagons and, increasingly, motor trucks; the teamsters' union was among the city's most powerful. Children sold newspapers on street corners, and pedlars sold ice, coal, rags and vegetables door to door.",
