    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Precinct captains, Hull House and the municipal court",
    text: "City neighbourhoods were run as much by political machines as by city hall. A precinct captain knew every family on his blocks, found jobs, fixed problems with the police and delivered coal or a turkey in hard times, expecting votes in return. Settlement houses such as Jane Addams's Hull House, founded in 1889, offered clubs, classes, baths and nurseries to immigrant neighbours and pushed for reforms. Neighbours in crowded flats and two-flats shared stoops, alleys and gossip. Quarrels between them over noise, children or rent went to the police or to the Municipal Court of Chicago, founded in 1906, which created specialised branches for domestic relations and small claims.",
    wiki: ["Political machine", "Hull House", "Jane Addams"],
