    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Iceboxes, coal stoves and the corner grocery",
    text: "In a working-class city flat of the early 1900s, the wife ran the household on a tight budget. She cooked on a coal or, increasingly, gas stove, with coal bought by the bushel or ton and carried up from the cellar; heating a flat through a Midwest winter was a large expense. Food was bought almost daily from the corner grocery or butcher, often on credit in a passbook, because the icebox, kept cold by blocks from the iceman, held little. Canned goods, bread from bakeries and cheap cuts of meat were staples, alongside the foods of each immigrant group. Many families took in boarders or did piecework at the kitchen table to make ends meet.",
