    title: "National parishes, storefront churches and saints' feasts",
    text: "In industrial cities, religion was organised by neighbourhood and nationality. Catholic Poles, Lithuanians, Italians and Germans built their own national parishes, with sermons in their language, parish schools and societies, while Jews built synagogues and Black migrants from the South founded Baptist and Pentecostal congregations, many of them in storefronts. Italians carried their village patron saints through the streets on feast days, pinning money to the statues. Catholic workers wore medals of Saint Joseph or Saint Christopher and lit candles before a shrine for help with work or a journey. Churches provided relief, insurance societies and a place of belonging in a new country.",
