    title: "Funeral homes, burial insurance and mothers' pensions",
    text: "Death in the industrial city came often through accidents at work, tuberculosis and, in 1918, influenza. Families paid a few cents a week to an insurance agent for an industrial burial policy, or joined a lodge or ethnic mutual aid society, so that no one would be buried by the county in a pauper's grave. Undertakers moved from laying out the body at home to embalming it in a funeral parlour. Widows kept up the grave, had masses said or observed yahrzeit, and hung photographs. Many widows with children were left destitute; in 1911 Illinois passed the first statewide mothers' pension law, a monthly allowance to keep children at home.",
