    title: "Building and loan, the bungalow and the loan shark",
    text: "For newcomers to the industrial city, from Poland, Italy or Mississippi, the first home was often a rented room with relatives or boarders near the mills. The dream of a couple was a house of their own, and immigrant neighbourhoods founded building and loan associations where members saved weekly and borrowed in turn to buy a two-flat or a brick bungalow; by the 1920s Chicago's bungalow belt stretched for miles. Those without savings borrowed from pawnbrokers, bought furniture on the instalment plan, or went to 'salary lenders' charging huge interest, who hounded debtors at work. When layoffs came, as in 1921 or after 1929, many families lost homes they had nearly paid for.",
