    title: "Painless dentists and the free dental dispensary",
    text: "By 1900 dentistry was a licensed profession with its own colleges, and American dentists were famous for gold fillings and crowns, but few working families could pay for regular care. A toothache usually meant a visit to a cheap storefront dentist advertising 'painless' extraction with nitrous oxide or cocaine, or pulling at home with a string. Sugar and white bread had made decay nearly universal. Reformers, alarmed by the teeth of schoolchildren, opened free dental dispensaries in settlement houses and public schools in the 1910s, and toothbrushes and toothpaste in tubes, pushed by advertising, spread through city households in the 1920s.",
