    text: "In Jamaica's mountains the Maroons, communities of people who had escaped slavery, lived by farming and by hunting wild hogs with dogs and lances, jerking the meat over pimento-wood fires, a technique that gave the island jerk pork; after the treaties of 1739 they held their own lands. On the estates, enslaved people fished the rivers and reefs with pots and nets for their own food and for sale, and some were full-time fishermen for the planter. Along the coast, droghers, small sailing boats crewed by enslaved and free Black sailors, carried hogsheads of sugar and puncheons of rum from estate wharves to the ports, and turtlers brought green turtles from the Cayman Islands.",
