    text: "Enslaved Jamaicans fed themselves largely from provision grounds, plots in the hills where they grew yams, plantains, cocoyams and vegetables on their one free day, and they sold the surplus at Sunday markets in Kingston, Spanish Town and the country towns. By the late 1700s they handled much of the island's small coin and food trade; women higglers bought and resold across the island. Markets were where news, rumours of rebellion and word of distant kin spread, beyond any newspaper. Enslaved people lent and borrowed among themselves, and some saved to buy freedom. But a planter's own debts were a constant danger: estates were seized and enslaved people sold to pay creditors, breaking families apart.",
