    text: "Enslaved Jamaicans drew on the religions of West and West-Central Africa. The obeah practitioner used charms, bundles and herbal knowledge to heal, to protect, to find thieves or to harm, and was feared by planters, who made obeah a crime after Tacky's Revolt of 1760. Myal was a collective dance and possession cult against evil spirits. Offerings to the spirits and the ancestors were poured at graves and cotton trees, and a worker might wear a charm for luck in a dangerous task. The Moravians came in 1754, and Black Baptist preachers from the 1780s. For pain, including toothache, people went to 'doctor women' in the estate hothouse, who used bush remedies.",
